window.UNITY_DOCS_INDEX = [{"title": "ATLAS Analysis on Unity", "url": "index.html", "text": "UMass Unity provides the compute resources. CVMFS supplies the ATLAS software. An Enterprise Linux 9 container brings them together on Unity's Ubuntu hosts. \n This guide documents how to use Unity as an analysis facility for ATLAS, from an interactive shell to a reproducible analysis environment. \n \n Start here \n Setting up the environment → \n Request CPU or GPU resources, enter an EL9 container, and load the software needed for your analysis. Includes optional shell shortcuts and checks for common setup problems. \n Submitting jobs with Slurm → \n Turn the tested interactive environment into a batch job. Includes downloadable scripts, a PyROOT smoke test, resource requests, logs, and troubleshooting. \n \n How the environment fits together \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Component \n Its role \n \n \n \n \n Unity login node \n Connect, edit files, and request resources. \n \n \n Slurm allocation \n Reserve CPUs, memory, time, and optional GPUs on a compute node. \n \n \n Apptainer container \n Provide the EL9 userspace required by the ATLAS release. \n \n \n CVMFS \n Deliver centrally maintained software and container images. \n \n \n Your work directory \n Keep analysis code, builds, inputs, and outputs on writable Unity storage. \n \n \n \n Start the container  after  the allocation has started. The container runs within that allocation; it does not reserve additional resources. \n About this guide \n The first two chapters cover interactive environment setup and Slurm batch jobs. Further chapters will cover data access and authentication, analysis workflows, and notebooks and machine learning as those procedures are established. \n The  Unity documentation  is the reference for cluster access, storage, and scheduling. The  ATLAS Software Documentation  is the reference for ATLAS software and analysis procedures. \n \n Validation status.  Rafael has tested the interactive environment recipe successfully on Unity, including access to  /work  without an explicit mount. The new batch recipe has passed local checks and awaits its first end-to-end Unity test. \n \n Found a problem or tested a setup?  Open an issue  with the relevant command, error, and compute-node hostname. Omit credentials and tokens. \n"}, {"title": "How the environment fits together", "url": "index.html#how-the-environment-fits-together", "text": "How the environment fits together \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Component \n Its role \n \n \n \n \n Unity login node \n Connect, edit files, and request resources. \n \n \n Slurm allocation \n Reserve CPUs, memory, time, and optional GPUs on a compute node. \n \n \n Apptainer container \n Provide the EL9 userspace required by the ATLAS release. \n \n \n CVMFS \n Deliver centrally maintained software and container images. \n \n \n Your work directory \n Keep analysis code, builds, inputs, and outputs on writable Unity storage. \n \n \n \n Start the container  after  the allocation has started. The container runs within that allocation; it does not reserve additional resources. \n"}, {"title": "About this guide", "url": "index.html#about-this-guide", "text": "About this guide \n The first two chapters cover interactive environment setup and Slurm batch jobs. Further chapters will cover data access and authentication, analysis workflows, and notebooks and machine learning as those procedures are established. \n The  Unity documentation  is the reference for cluster access, storage, and scheduling. The  ATLAS Software Documentation  is the reference for ATLAS software and analysis procedures. \n \n Validation status.  Rafael has tested the interactive environment recipe successfully on Unity, including access to  /work  without an explicit mount. The new batch recipe has passed local checks and awaits its first end-to-end Unity test. \n \n Found a problem or tested a setup?  Open an issue  with the relevant command, error, and compute-node hostname. Omit credentials and tokens. \n"}, {"title": "Setting up the environment", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html", "text": "Use a Slurm allocation for your work, then start an EL9 container on the allocated compute node. Inside it, ATLAS software is available through CVMFS without a local installation. \n \n Before you begin:  you need a Unity account, access to a group work directory, and a Bash shell. CVMFS must be accessible on the  compute node , not just the login node. Rafael has tested this interactive recipe successfully on Unity. \n \n 1. Connect to Unity \n From your own computer, connect using your Unity username: \n # Your computer — replace YOUR_UNITY_USERNAME.\nssh YOUR_UNITY_USERNAME@login.unityhpc.org \n Follow Unity's  SSH guide  for account and SSH-key setup. Use the login node to prepare and submit work; run builds, event processing, and training on allocated compute nodes. \n 2. Request compute resources \n Choose  one  of the following commands on the login node. Adjust the resource request to your workload. \n CPU session \n # Unity login node\nsalloc --partition=cpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 \\\n       --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \n GPU session \n # Unity login node — alternative to the CPU request\nsalloc --partition=gpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --gpus=1 \\\n       --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Option \n Meaning in these examples \n \n \n \n \n --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 \n One node and one task. \n \n \n --cpus-per-task=4 \n Four CPU cores for that task. Configure your application's threads accordingly. \n \n \n --mem=16G \n 16 GB of host memory for the node allocation; this is not GPU memory. \n \n \n --time=02:00:00 \n A two-hour wall-time limit. \n \n \n --gpus=1 \n One GPU, only in the GPU example. \n \n \n \n Unity's documented  salloc  workflow opens a shell on the compute node once resources are available. Confirm your location: \n # After the allocation is granted\nhostname -f\nprintf 'Job: %s\\nNodes: %s\\n' \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" \"$SLURM_JOB_NODELIST\"\nuname -m \n The hostname should be a compute node in your allocation. If you are still on a login node with an active allocation, use  srun --pty bash -l  to start a shell on the allocated node. Do not run a second  salloc  inside the first one. \n This guide targets  x86_64  nodes. Containers do not translate CPU architectures; ARM and POWER partitions need compatible software and images.  gpu-preempt  is an alternative when interruption is acceptable. Consult the current  partition list  and  GPU guide  before choosing constraints or preemptible resources. \n 3. Prepare the host environment \n Run the following in the  Ubuntu compute-node shell , before entering a container: \n module load apptainer/latest\napptainer --version\n\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nalias setupATLAS='source ${ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE}/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh'\n\nls -l \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \n The last command must find the setup script. Stop here if it fails: an empty  /cvmfs  directory alone does not establish that the ATLAS repository is available. Report the compute-node hostname and the missing path to Unity support. The first access to a CVMFS path may take longer while content is fetched. \n Start from a clean host shell. Deactivate any Conda or Python virtual environment and avoid loading Ubuntu ROOT, Python, or compiler modules for use inside the EL9 environment. Load the Apptainer runtime on the host; select analysis tools inside the container. \n Choose a work directory \n Replace  pi_YOUR_PI  with your actual Unity group directory. Do not run the placeholder unchanged. \n # Ubuntu compute node — edit the group directory first\nexport ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/pi_YOUR_PI/$USER/atlas\"\nmkdir -p \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\npwd -P \n Use  /work  or a workspace allocated through Unity's  scratch service  for active job I/O. Keep shell configuration in your home directory. Consult the  storage guide  for quotas, retention, and moving data from longer-term storage. \n 4. Enter the EL9 container \n For a CPU session, run: \n # Ubuntu compute node, in your analysis directory\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n -c el9  selects the ATLAS EL9 environment. On the tested Unity setup,  /work  is already visible inside the container without  -m /work , so no explicit mount is needed for the standard workflow. File permissions still apply. \n Use  -m  only when a directory you need is  not already visible  inside the container. For example, if  /scratch  is missing, leave the container and relaunch with  setupATLAS -c el9 -m /scratch . Check the paths you need after entering; automatic mounts can depend on the launcher and site configuration.  setupATLAS -c -h  describes additional mount options. \n The launcher initializes the ATLAS user interface in the container. Check it before selecting a release: \n # Inside the EL9 container\ncat /etc/os-release\nuname -m\npwd\ntype lsetup\ntype asetup\nls /cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo\nls /work \n Expect an EL9 distribution and  x86_64 . A container shares the host kernel, so  uname -r  is  not  a test of its Linux distribution. The launcher may map the working directory to  /srv  and use a different home path. Check  pwd  and access to your  /work/...  directory. Host variables such as  ATLAS_WORKDIR  need not be preserved. \n If  lsetup  is unavailable despite a successful container launch, initialize the interface explicitly  inside  that container: \n export ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \n \n Two separate choices.  The container selects the operating-system environment.  asetup  selects an ATLAS software release. Entering EL9 does not by itself load AnalysisBase, AthAnalysis, or Athena. \n \n 5. Load analysis software \n Choose the environment needed by your analysis. Use a fresh container session when switching between unrelated software stacks. \n A standalone ROOT check \n In an EL9 session with no analysis release loaded: \n lsetup root\nroot-config --version\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n This checks a basic ROOT/PyROOT environment. For reproducible work, select an explicit tool version using the choices shown by  lsetup \"root --help\" , and record it with your analysis. \n AnalysisBase, AthAnalysis, or Athena \n For an analysis release, begin in a  fresh EL9 session  and select the exact project and version required by your analysis, following the ATLAS  release setup guide . For example, this prompts for a version instead of silently choosing a moving default: \n # Inside a fresh EL9 container, in your analysis directory\nread -r -p 'AnalysisBase release required by your analysis: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ -n \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" ]]; then\n    asetup \"AnalysisBase,${ATLAS_RELEASE}\"\nfi \n Use  AthAnalysis  or  Athena  in place of  AnalysisBase  when that is what your workflow requires. Check the setup output for a successful release selection and an EL9-compatible platform. Do not guess a release number from this guide; the analysis determines the supported release. \n After a successful setup: \n printf 'Project: %s\\nVersion: %s\\nPlatform: %s\\n' \\\n       \"$AtlasProject\" \"$AtlasVersion\" \"$BINARY_TAG\"\ncommand -v python\nroot-config --version\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n Use the ROOT and Python supplied by the release. Keep standalone  lsetup root  and release setup in separate sessions. Record the project, version, platform, and container information with your code; record the resolved image from the launcher output if you need to reproduce the precise container as well. \n 6. Enable GPU access \n For an  NVIDIA GPU allocation , use this launch sequence in place of the CPU container command. First check the host: \n # Ubuntu GPU compute node, after salloc and host preparation\nnvidia-smi\nprintf 'CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=%s\\n' \"${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-<unset>}\" \n Apptainer's  APPTAINER_NV  enables NVIDIA device and driver-library passthrough, corresponding to  --nv . Pass Slurm's device selection into the clean container environment as well: \n # Ubuntu GPU compute node\nexport APPTAINER_NV=true\nif [[ -n ${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-} ]]; then\n    export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"$CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"\nfi\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n Then check  inside  the container: \n nvidia-smi\nprintf 'CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=%s\\n' \"${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-<unset>}\" \n Check the launcher's output to confirm it uses Apptainer. If it selects a different runtime or the GPU check fails, inspect  setupATLAS -c -h  and the  Apptainer GPU documentation  before proceeding. Verify device visibility with the GPU framework you intend to use. \n GPU visibility does not install a CUDA-enabled analysis framework, or make ordinary ATLAS code use the GPU. A compatible GPU software stack is a separate choice. Do not override Slurm's device selection or set it to  all ;  nvidia-smi  may enumerate more devices than your application is assigned. Keep these GPU exports out of your general  .bashrc  and remove them in the host shell after leaving the container: \n # Back in the Ubuntu compute-node shell after exiting the container\nunset APPTAINER_NV APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES \n 7. Add optional shell shortcuts \n Add the following to  your existing   ~/.bashrc  on Unity. These lines define commands; they do not allocate resources or start software at login. The same snippet is available as  atlas-bashrc.sh . \n export ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\n\n# Shortcuts for interactive Bash sessions only.\nif [[ $- == *i* ]]; then\n    alias setupATLAS='source ${ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE}/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh'\n    alias unity_cpu='salloc --partition=cpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00'\n    alias unity_gpu='salloc --partition=gpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --gpus=1 --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00'\nfi \n Reload it in the  host shell  with  source ~/.bashrc . You can then use  unity_cpu  or  unity_gpu  on the login node, followed by the host preparation and container commands on the allocated node. \n You may also add your edited  ATLAS_WORKDIR  export. Keep  module load ,  setupATLAS -c el9 ,  asetup , and  lsetup  as explicit commands rather than executing them automatically from  .bashrc . This avoids unexpected containers or incompatible software environments when starting new shells. In scripts, source  atlasLocalSetup.sh  explicitly; non-interactive Bash does not normally expand aliases. \n 8. End or reconnect to a session \n Run  exit  inside the container to return to the Ubuntu compute-node shell. Run  exit  again to leave the interactive allocation and release its resources. A new allocation requires a new container and software setup; files in your work directory remain. \n For a session that survives an SSH disconnect, start  tmux new -s atlas   on the login node before  salloc . Detach with  Ctrl+B , then  D . Reconnect to the same login node and use  tmux attach -t atlas . Record that login hostname before starting.  tmux  does not extend the Slurm wall-time limit or prevent preemption. See Unity's  interactive-job guidance . \n Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n setupATLAS: command not found \n Define the export and alias in the current Bash shell, or source the setup script explicitly. \n \n \n Missing  atlasLocalSetup.sh \n Test its full CVMFS path on the allocated compute node; ask Unity support if unavailable. \n \n \n apptainer: command not found \n Load  apptainer/latest  on the host. Use  module avail apptainer  if the module name changes. \n \n \n Container fails with namespace or mount errors \n Save the launcher output and hostname for support; node/runtime configuration may need adjustment. \n \n \n Work files disappear inside the container \n Add the correct host path with  -m , including a symlink's real target; verify with  pwd -P  before launch. \n \n \n Container still reports Ubuntu \n Check  /etc/os-release ; the container may have failed to start, leaving you in the host shell. \n \n \n ROOT/Python errors after setup \n Try a fresh container, choose one software stack, and check the release's platform. \n \n \n GPU works on host but not in container \n Confirm an NVIDIA allocation, Apptainer passthrough, and preservation of Slurm's device selection. \n \n \n Allocation is pending \n Inspect  squeue -u \"$USER\"  on the host; reduce an unnecessarily large request or consult current partition availability. \n \n \n \n References \n \n ATLAS Software Documentation :  environment prerequisites  and  user-interface setup . \n Unity documentation :  interactive CLI jobs ,  container support ,  partitions , and  storage . \n US ATLAS Analysis Facilities: Apptainer usage . \n Apptainer GPU support  and  environment-variable reference . \n \n Next:   Submitting jobs with Slurm  recreates this environment automatically on a batch worker. \n Documentation reviewed:  7 November 2026 . Rafael reported a successful test of the interactive recipe, including  /work  visibility without an explicit mount . The exact node, release, and GPU framework were not recorded; check those for your own analysis. \n"}, {"title": "1. Connect to Unity", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#connect", "text": "1. Connect to Unity \n From your own computer, connect using your Unity username: \n # Your computer — replace YOUR_UNITY_USERNAME.\nssh YOUR_UNITY_USERNAME@login.unityhpc.org \n Follow Unity's  SSH guide  for account and SSH-key setup. Use the login node to prepare and submit work; run builds, event processing, and training on allocated compute nodes. \n"}, {"title": "2. Request compute resources", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#allocate", "text": "2. Request compute resources \n Choose  one  of the following commands on the login node. Adjust the resource request to your workload. \n CPU session \n # Unity login node\nsalloc --partition=cpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 \\\n       --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \n GPU session \n # Unity login node — alternative to the CPU request\nsalloc --partition=gpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --gpus=1 \\\n       --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Option \n Meaning in these examples \n \n \n \n \n --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 \n One node and one task. \n \n \n --cpus-per-task=4 \n Four CPU cores for that task. Configure your application's threads accordingly. \n \n \n --mem=16G \n 16 GB of host memory for the node allocation; this is not GPU memory. \n \n \n --time=02:00:00 \n A two-hour wall-time limit. \n \n \n --gpus=1 \n One GPU, only in the GPU example. \n \n \n \n Unity's documented  salloc  workflow opens a shell on the compute node once resources are available. Confirm your location: \n # After the allocation is granted\nhostname -f\nprintf 'Job: %s\\nNodes: %s\\n' \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" \"$SLURM_JOB_NODELIST\"\nuname -m \n The hostname should be a compute node in your allocation. If you are still on a login node with an active allocation, use  srun --pty bash -l  to start a shell on the allocated node. Do not run a second  salloc  inside the first one. \n This guide targets  x86_64  nodes. Containers do not translate CPU architectures; ARM and POWER partitions need compatible software and images.  gpu-preempt  is an alternative when interruption is acceptable. Consult the current  partition list  and  GPU guide  before choosing constraints or preemptible resources. \n"}, {"title": "3. Prepare the host environment", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#prepare-host", "text": "3. Prepare the host environment \n Run the following in the  Ubuntu compute-node shell , before entering a container: \n module load apptainer/latest\napptainer --version\n\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nalias setupATLAS='source ${ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE}/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh'\n\nls -l \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \n The last command must find the setup script. Stop here if it fails: an empty  /cvmfs  directory alone does not establish that the ATLAS repository is available. Report the compute-node hostname and the missing path to Unity support. The first access to a CVMFS path may take longer while content is fetched. \n Start from a clean host shell. Deactivate any Conda or Python virtual environment and avoid loading Ubuntu ROOT, Python, or compiler modules for use inside the EL9 environment. Load the Apptainer runtime on the host; select analysis tools inside the container. \n Choose a work directory \n Replace  pi_YOUR_PI  with your actual Unity group directory. Do not run the placeholder unchanged. \n # Ubuntu compute node — edit the group directory first\nexport ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/pi_YOUR_PI/$USER/atlas\"\nmkdir -p \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\npwd -P \n Use  /work  or a workspace allocated through Unity's  scratch service  for active job I/O. Keep shell configuration in your home directory. Consult the  storage guide  for quotas, retention, and moving data from longer-term storage. \n"}, {"title": "4. Enter the EL9 container", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#container", "text": "4. Enter the EL9 container \n For a CPU session, run: \n # Ubuntu compute node, in your analysis directory\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n -c el9  selects the ATLAS EL9 environment. On the tested Unity setup,  /work  is already visible inside the container without  -m /work , so no explicit mount is needed for the standard workflow. File permissions still apply. \n Use  -m  only when a directory you need is  not already visible  inside the container. For example, if  /scratch  is missing, leave the container and relaunch with  setupATLAS -c el9 -m /scratch . Check the paths you need after entering; automatic mounts can depend on the launcher and site configuration.  setupATLAS -c -h  describes additional mount options. \n The launcher initializes the ATLAS user interface in the container. Check it before selecting a release: \n # Inside the EL9 container\ncat /etc/os-release\nuname -m\npwd\ntype lsetup\ntype asetup\nls /cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo\nls /work \n Expect an EL9 distribution and  x86_64 . A container shares the host kernel, so  uname -r  is  not  a test of its Linux distribution. The launcher may map the working directory to  /srv  and use a different home path. Check  pwd  and access to your  /work/...  directory. Host variables such as  ATLAS_WORKDIR  need not be preserved. \n If  lsetup  is unavailable despite a successful container launch, initialize the interface explicitly  inside  that container: \n export ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \n \n Two separate choices.  The container selects the operating-system environment.  asetup  selects an ATLAS software release. Entering EL9 does not by itself load AnalysisBase, AthAnalysis, or Athena. \n \n"}, {"title": "5. Load analysis software", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#software", "text": "5. Load analysis software \n Choose the environment needed by your analysis. Use a fresh container session when switching between unrelated software stacks. \n A standalone ROOT check \n In an EL9 session with no analysis release loaded: \n lsetup root\nroot-config --version\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n This checks a basic ROOT/PyROOT environment. For reproducible work, select an explicit tool version using the choices shown by  lsetup \"root --help\" , and record it with your analysis. \n AnalysisBase, AthAnalysis, or Athena \n For an analysis release, begin in a  fresh EL9 session  and select the exact project and version required by your analysis, following the ATLAS  release setup guide . For example, this prompts for a version instead of silently choosing a moving default: \n # Inside a fresh EL9 container, in your analysis directory\nread -r -p 'AnalysisBase release required by your analysis: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ -n \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" ]]; then\n    asetup \"AnalysisBase,${ATLAS_RELEASE}\"\nfi \n Use  AthAnalysis  or  Athena  in place of  AnalysisBase  when that is what your workflow requires. Check the setup output for a successful release selection and an EL9-compatible platform. Do not guess a release number from this guide; the analysis determines the supported release. \n After a successful setup: \n printf 'Project: %s\\nVersion: %s\\nPlatform: %s\\n' \\\n       \"$AtlasProject\" \"$AtlasVersion\" \"$BINARY_TAG\"\ncommand -v python\nroot-config --version\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n Use the ROOT and Python supplied by the release. Keep standalone  lsetup root  and release setup in separate sessions. Record the project, version, platform, and container information with your code; record the resolved image from the launcher output if you need to reproduce the precise container as well. \n"}, {"title": "6. Enable GPU access", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#gpu", "text": "6. Enable GPU access \n For an  NVIDIA GPU allocation , use this launch sequence in place of the CPU container command. First check the host: \n # Ubuntu GPU compute node, after salloc and host preparation\nnvidia-smi\nprintf 'CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=%s\\n' \"${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-<unset>}\" \n Apptainer's  APPTAINER_NV  enables NVIDIA device and driver-library passthrough, corresponding to  --nv . Pass Slurm's device selection into the clean container environment as well: \n # Ubuntu GPU compute node\nexport APPTAINER_NV=true\nif [[ -n ${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-} ]]; then\n    export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"$CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"\nfi\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n Then check  inside  the container: \n nvidia-smi\nprintf 'CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=%s\\n' \"${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-<unset>}\" \n Check the launcher's output to confirm it uses Apptainer. If it selects a different runtime or the GPU check fails, inspect  setupATLAS -c -h  and the  Apptainer GPU documentation  before proceeding. Verify device visibility with the GPU framework you intend to use. \n GPU visibility does not install a CUDA-enabled analysis framework, or make ordinary ATLAS code use the GPU. A compatible GPU software stack is a separate choice. Do not override Slurm's device selection or set it to  all ;  nvidia-smi  may enumerate more devices than your application is assigned. Keep these GPU exports out of your general  .bashrc  and remove them in the host shell after leaving the container: \n # Back in the Ubuntu compute-node shell after exiting the container\nunset APPTAINER_NV APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES \n"}, {"title": "7. Add optional shell shortcuts", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#bashrc", "text": "7. Add optional shell shortcuts \n Add the following to  your existing   ~/.bashrc  on Unity. These lines define commands; they do not allocate resources or start software at login. The same snippet is available as  atlas-bashrc.sh . \n export ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\n\n# Shortcuts for interactive Bash sessions only.\nif [[ $- == *i* ]]; then\n    alias setupATLAS='source ${ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE}/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh'\n    alias unity_cpu='salloc --partition=cpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00'\n    alias unity_gpu='salloc --partition=gpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --gpus=1 --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00'\nfi \n Reload it in the  host shell  with  source ~/.bashrc . You can then use  unity_cpu  or  unity_gpu  on the login node, followed by the host preparation and container commands on the allocated node. \n You may also add your edited  ATLAS_WORKDIR  export. Keep  module load ,  setupATLAS -c el9 ,  asetup , and  lsetup  as explicit commands rather than executing them automatically from  .bashrc . This avoids unexpected containers or incompatible software environments when starting new shells. In scripts, source  atlasLocalSetup.sh  explicitly; non-interactive Bash does not normally expand aliases. \n"}, {"title": "8. End or reconnect to a session", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#sessions", "text": "8. End or reconnect to a session \n Run  exit  inside the container to return to the Ubuntu compute-node shell. Run  exit  again to leave the interactive allocation and release its resources. A new allocation requires a new container and software setup; files in your work directory remain. \n For a session that survives an SSH disconnect, start  tmux new -s atlas   on the login node before  salloc . Detach with  Ctrl+B , then  D . Reconnect to the same login node and use  tmux attach -t atlas . Record that login hostname before starting.  tmux  does not extend the Slurm wall-time limit or prevent preemption. See Unity's  interactive-job guidance . \n"}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#troubleshooting", "text": "Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n setupATLAS: command not found \n Define the export and alias in the current Bash shell, or source the setup script explicitly. \n \n \n Missing  atlasLocalSetup.sh \n Test its full CVMFS path on the allocated compute node; ask Unity support if unavailable. \n \n \n apptainer: command not found \n Load  apptainer/latest  on the host. Use  module avail apptainer  if the module name changes. \n \n \n Container fails with namespace or mount errors \n Save the launcher output and hostname for support; node/runtime configuration may need adjustment. \n \n \n Work files disappear inside the container \n Add the correct host path with  -m , including a symlink's real target; verify with  pwd -P  before launch. \n \n \n Container still reports Ubuntu \n Check  /etc/os-release ; the container may have failed to start, leaving you in the host shell. \n \n \n ROOT/Python errors after setup \n Try a fresh container, choose one software stack, and check the release's platform. \n \n \n GPU works on host but not in container \n Confirm an NVIDIA allocation, Apptainer passthrough, and preservation of Slurm's device selection. \n \n \n Allocation is pending \n Inspect  squeue -u \"$USER\"  on the host; reduce an unnecessarily large request or consult current partition availability. \n \n \n \n"}, {"title": "References", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#references", "text": "References \n \n ATLAS Software Documentation :  environment prerequisites  and  user-interface setup . \n Unity documentation :  interactive CLI jobs ,  container support ,  partitions , and  storage . \n US ATLAS Analysis Facilities: Apptainer usage . \n Apptainer GPU support  and  environment-variable reference . \n \n Next:   Submitting jobs with Slurm  recreates this environment automatically on a batch worker. \n Documentation reviewed:  7 November 2026 . Rafael reported a successful test of the interactive recipe, including  /work  visibility without an explicit mount . The exact node, release, and GPU framework were not recorded; check those for your own analysis. \n"}, {"title": "Submitting jobs with Slurm", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html", "text": "A batch job needs the same environment as an interactive session. Slurm starts the job on an allocated  Ubuntu compute node ; the job loads Apptainer, enters  EL9 , selects an ATLAS release, and runs the analysis there. \n You submit with  sbatch  from a Unity login node. There is no preceding  salloc , and you can disconnect after submission. The batch allocation and its wall-time limit are managed by Slurm. \n \n Prerequisite:  first complete  Setting up the environment  and choose an exact ATLAS release that works for your analysis. Rafael has tested that interactive recipe on Unity. The batch example below has passed local syntax and simulated execution checks; its first end-to-end Unity run is still pending. \n \n 1. Separate scheduling from the analysis \n The example uses two files: \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n File \n Where it runs \n What it does \n \n \n \n \n atlas-job.sbatch \n Ubuntu worker node \n Requests resources through  #SBATCH  directives, loads the container runtime, and starts the EL9 command. \n \n \n run-atlas.sh \n Inside the EL9 container \n Initializes ATLAS, selects the requested release, and runs a small PyROOT test. \n \n \n \n The key ATLAS option is  -r , which runs a command inside the container without opening an interactive prompt. The wrapper invokes the setup script directly: \n # Pattern used in the batch script; the variables are defined there.\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \\\n    -c el9 -r \"$unity_atlas_command\" \n This is the scripted equivalent of  setupATLAS -c el9 , followed by commands inside the container. Simply writing  setupATLAS -c el9  on one line and your analysis on the next does not put that next line inside the container. Keep the analysis in the command passed to  -r . \n The  US ATLAS benchmarking examples  use this non-interactive interface. Our wrapper adapts it to Unity's module and Slurm configuration. \n 2. Prepare a shared job directory \n Start from a  fresh Ubuntu login shell , outside an interactive ATLAS container. Replace  pi_YOUR_PI  with your actual Unity group directory, as in the first chapter: \n # Unity login node\nexport ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/pi_YOUR_PI/$USER/atlas\"\nmkdir -p \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/atlas-test\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/atlas-test\"\nmkdir -p logs\n\ncurl --fail --location --output atlas-job.sbatch \\\n    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/slurm/atlas-job.sbatch\ncurl --fail --location --output run-atlas.sh \\\n    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/slurm/run-atlas.sh \n You can also save the files directly:  atlas-job.sbatch  and  run-atlas.sh . Both belong in the directory from which you will submit. No executable permission is required here: Slurm reads the submission script, and the container runs the payload with  bash . \n Create  logs/  before submitting.  Slurm opens the output files before the script runs, so creating that directory inside the job would be too late. \n Slurm transfers the submission script, but it does  not  transfer your payload, analysis code, input files, or software build. They must remain accessible on shared storage. Avoid editing  run-atlas.sh  or its dependencies while jobs using them are queued or running; keep a separate copy for a new version. \n 3. The worker-node script \n atlas-job.sbatch  requests one CPU, 4 GB of memory, and 15 minutes for the small test. Its login Bash shell initializes the host's module environment. It creates a separate  runs/JOB_ID/  directory for every submission. \n #!/bin/bash -l\n#SBATCH --job-name=atlas-test\n#SBATCH --partition=cpu\n#SBATCH --nodes=1\n#SBATCH --ntasks=1\n#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=1\n#SBATCH --mem=4G\n#SBATCH --time=00:15:00\n#SBATCH --output=logs/%x-%j.out\n#SBATCH --error=logs/%x-%j.err\n#SBATCH --export=NONE\n\n# This script runs on the allocated Ubuntu worker node.\nif [[ $# -ne 1 || $1 != *,* ]]; then\n    echo \"Usage: sbatch atlas-job.sbatch AnalysisBase,VERSION\" >&2\n    exit 2\nfi\nunity_atlas_release=$1\ncd \"${SLURM_SUBMIT_DIR:?Submit from your shared job directory}\" || exit 1\nunity_atlas_payload=\"$PWD/run-atlas.sh\"\nif [[ ! -r \"$unity_atlas_payload\" ]]; then\n    echo \"Cannot read payload: $unity_atlas_payload\" >&2\n    exit 1\nfi\nunity_atlas_run_dir=\"$PWD/runs/${SLURM_JOB_ID:?Missing Slurm job ID}\"\nmkdir -p \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" || exit 1\n\n# Load the container runtime on the host, before entering EL9.\nmodule load apptainer/latest || exit \"$?\"\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nif [[ ! -r \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" ]]; then\n    echo \"ATLASLocalRootBase is unavailable on $(hostname)\" >&2\n    exit 1\nfi\n\n# Enable NVIDIA passthrough only when this job has allocated GPUs.\nif [[ -n ${SLURM_JOB_GPUS:-} ]]; then\n    export APPTAINER_NV=true\n    if [[ -n ${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-} ]]; then\n        export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"$CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"\n    fi\nfi\n\n# Pass arguments explicitly; do not depend on host variables surviving\n# the container's environment filtering. %q quotes each Bash argument.\nprintf -v unity_atlas_command 'bash %q %q %q %q' \\\n    \"$unity_atlas_payload\" \"$unity_atlas_release\" \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" \\\n    \"${SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK:-1}\"\nprintf 'Job %s on %s; release %s; output %s\\n' \\\n    \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" \"$(hostname)\" \"$unity_atlas_release\" \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\"\ncd \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" || exit 1\n\n# -r runs the payload inside the container and then leaves the container.\n# /work is already visible on the tested Unity setup; no -m is needed.\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \\\n    -c el9 -r \"$unity_atlas_command\"\nunity_atlas_status=$?\nprintf 'Container command exited with status %s\\n' \"$unity_atlas_status\"\nexit \"$unity_atlas_status\" \n The wrapper sources ATLASLocalRootBase explicitly instead of depending on the interactive  setupATLAS  alias in  .bashrc . The  --export=NONE  directive avoids importing the submission shell's full environment; Slurm still supplies its job variables and may initialize the user's login environment. This is not a completely empty environment. Keep software and container startup out of your shell startup files, as described in the first chapter. \n /work  is already visible inside the tested Unity container, so the standard command needs no  -m /work . If you use another filesystem that is missing inside EL9, add the corresponding  -m /actual/path  to the container launch. \n This is a  single-node, single-process  job. Slurm already runs the batch script on the worker, so it does not need an additional  srun  for this example. MPI and multiple simultaneous tasks require a different launch pattern. If you later introduce  srun , note that this script uses  --export=NONE : use  srun --export=ALL ...  when the step needs the environment you have prepared. \n 4. The script inside EL9 \n run-atlas.sh  starts a fresh Bash process inside the container. It initializes the ATLAS command definitions, runs  asetup  for the release passed at submission, and creates  hist.root  without requiring any input data or credentials. \n #!/bin/bash\n# This script runs inside the EL9 container.\nunity_atlas_release=${1:?Missing ATLAS project and version}\nunity_atlas_run_dir=${2:?Missing output directory}\nunity_atlas_cpus=${3:-1}\ncd \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" || exit 1\n# Do not let a sourced setup script interpret the payload's arguments.\nset --\n\n# A new Bash process needs its own ATLAS command definitions.\n# ALRB defines aliases such as asetup; Bash scripts must enable them.\nshopt -s expand_aliases\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\"\nunity_atlas_status=$?\nif (( unity_atlas_status != 0 )); then\n    exit \"$unity_atlas_status\"\nfi\nasetup \"$unity_atlas_release\"\nunity_atlas_status=$?\nif (( unity_atlas_status != 0 )); then\n    exit \"$unity_atlas_status\"\nfi\n\n# Enable strict error handling after the external setup scripts finish.\nset -euo pipefail\nexport OMP_NUM_THREADS=\"$unity_atlas_cpus\"\nprintf 'Container OS:\\n'\ncat /etc/os-release\nprintf '\\nWorking directory: %s\\n' \"$PWD\"\n\n# Replace this smoke test with your analysis command.\npython -u - <<'PY'\nimport ROOT\n\nROOT.gROOT.SetBatch(True)\nROOT.gRandom.SetSeed(12345)\noutput = ROOT.TFile.Open(\"hist.root\", \"RECREATE\")\nif not output or output.IsZombie():\n    raise RuntimeError(\"Cannot create hist.root\")\nhist = ROOT.TH1D(\"example\", \"ATLAS on Unity; x; Entries\", 50, -5, 5)\nhist.FillRandom(\"gaus\", 10000)\nhist.Write()\noutput.Close()\nprint(\"ROOT version:\", ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())\nprint(\"Wrote hist.root with 10000 entries\")\nPY\ntest -s hist.root\nprintf 'Payload finished successfully.\\n' \n shopt -s expand_aliases  lets this non-interactive Bash script use the aliases defined by ATLASLocalRootBase, including  asetup . The setup and  asetup  commands are on separate lines so those definitions are available before the release command is parsed. \n External setup scripts run before strict Bash error handling is enabled; their return codes are checked explicitly. Once setup finishes,  set -euo pipefail  makes a failed analysis command stop the payload. The wrapper returns the container command's status to Slurm. When adapting the example, keep this error handling: a final successful  echo  must not hide a failed analysis. \n 5. Submit and inspect the result \n Use the  same project and exact release version  you successfully tested interactively, for example  AnalysisBase  followed by its version, separated by a comma. Do not enter a literal placeholder or a moving  latest  version. The prompt below runs on the login node; the batch job itself never prompts for input. \n # Still in the directory containing both scripts and logs/.\nread -r -p 'Tested ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" == *,* ]]; then\n    if JOB_ID=$(sbatch --parsable atlas-job.sbatch \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"); then\n        JOB_ID=${JOB_ID%%;*}\n        printf 'Submitted job: %s\\n' \"$JOB_ID\"\n    fi\nelse\n    echo 'Enter a project and exact version, separated by a comma.'\nfi \n Record the returned job ID. In a later login session, set  JOB_ID  to that number before using these commands: \n # Queued/running jobs; a completed job will disappear from squeue.\nsqueue --jobs=\"$JOB_ID\"\n\n# Accounting, including the batch step; may take a short time to update.\nsacct --jobs=\"$JOB_ID\" \\\n      --format=JobID,JobName,State,ExitCode,Elapsed,MaxRSS\n\n# These files appear once the job starts.\ntail -n 50 \"logs/atlas-test-${JOB_ID}.out\"\ntail -n 50 \"logs/atlas-test-${JOB_ID}.err\"\nls -lh \"runs/${JOB_ID}/hist.root\" \n For a successful smoke test, check  all three : \n \n Slurm reports  COMPLETED  with  ExitCode  equal to  0:0 . \n The output log identifies EL9, reports the ROOT version, and ends with the successful payload message and container status zero. \n runs/JOB_ID/hist.root  exists and is nonempty. In an interactive session with the same release, you can open it and inspect the histogram  example , which should have 10,000 entries. \n \n MaxRSS  is generally reported for a step such as  JOB_ID.batch ; keep those rows in the accounting output. Check both logs when a job fails. For Slurm, the first number in  ExitCode  is the return code and the second is the terminating signal. \n To cancel a queued or running job  when you want to stop it : \n scancel \"$JOB_ID\" \n 6. Replace the test with your analysis \n Replace the Python block and the  test -s hist.root  check in  run-atlas.sh  with your analysis command and an appropriate output check. Keep the release setup and run-directory change. Relative output paths then land in  runs/JOB_ID/ ; use absolute paths for shared inputs and code. \n For example, after changing these paths to your own: \n # Inside run-atlas.sh, after asetup and set -euo pipefail.\npython -u /work/pi_YOUR_PI/YOUR_USERNAME/atlas/analysis/run.py \\\n    --input /work/pi_YOUR_PI/shared/input.root \\\n    --output result.root\ntest -s result.root \n If your analysis uses a compiled ATLAS package, build it in an EL9 allocation with the same release and platform, then source that build's exact  setup.sh  after  asetup  in the payload. Keep the build accessible on shared storage. Do not build the same directory concurrently from multiple jobs. \n Remote data access and authentication need their own setup; having software on CVMFS does not grant access to protected data. The initial smoke test deliberately needs neither. \n Adjust CPU, memory, and time \n Command-line  sbatch  options override the corresponding  #SBATCH  directives. For example, once your analysis is configured to use four CPUs: \n sbatch --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \\\n    atlas-job.sbatch \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" \n Requesting four CPUs does not automatically parallelize Python or ROOT. The example passes the CPU allocation into the container and sets  OMP_NUM_THREADS ; configure your application's own thread or worker count as needed. Keep that count within the allocation.  --mem  here is memory per node, not per CPU. \n Keep directives at the top of the file, before shell commands. Slurm reads them before executing Bash, so  #SBATCH --output=$HOME/...  does not expand  $HOME . Use a literal path, a Slurm filename token such as  %j  (job ID), or an option on the  sbatch  command line. \n Request an NVIDIA GPU \n The same wrapper detects an allocated GPU through  SLURM_JOB_GPUS , enables Apptainer's NVIDIA support, and forwards Slurm's  CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES  selection when present. Request the GPU at submission: \n sbatch --partition=gpu --gpus=1 --cpus-per-task=4 \\\n    --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 atlas-job.sbatch \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" \n Add  nvidia-smi  after setup inside  run-atlas.sh  to inspect device visibility, then use a CUDA-capable application and compatible software environment. The provided histogram test runs on the CPU even when a GPU is allocated; it does not validate a GPU framework. Do not replace Slurm's device selection with a hard-coded GPU number. \n Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n No log files yet \n A pending job has not started. Check  squeue ; also confirm  logs/  existed before submission and is writable. \n \n \n module: command not found \n Keep the login-shell shebang  #!/bin/bash -l ; check host shell initialization and Unity's module instructions. \n \n \n setupATLAS  or  asetup  is not found \n Use the supplied explicit setup and alias-expansion sequence; an interactive  .bashrc  alias is not enough for batch scripts. \n \n \n Script or input missing inside the container \n Submit from the shared job directory, keep the payload there, and verify its absolute path inside EL9. Add  -m  only for paths not already visible. \n \n \n Software setup fails \n Confirm the exact project, release, and platform worked in a fresh interactive EL9 session. Check compute-node CVMFS access. \n \n \n FAILED ,  OUT_OF_MEMORY , or  TIMEOUT \n Read both logs and the  sacct  state; fix the command or adjust the measured memory/time requirement. \n \n \n COMPLETED  but output is missing \n Check that the payload actually ran, did not hide a failed command, and wrote to the intended run directory. Keep explicit output checks. \n \n \n GPU unavailable inside the payload \n Check the GPU resource request, NVIDIA passthrough, Slurm's device selection, and the application's CUDA support. \n \n \n \n References \n \n Unity: batch jobs with sbatch  and  partitions . \n US ATLAS analysis-facility benchmarking examples , including ATLASLocalRootBase container commands and batch wrappers. \n ATLAS Software Documentation . \n Slurm sbatch ,  sacct , and  job exit codes . \n Apptainer NVIDIA GPU support . \n \n Documentation reviewed:  7 November 2026 . Interactive Unity setup:  tested by Rafael . This batch recipe:  local checks passed; end-to-end Unity test pending . \n"}, {"title": "1. Separate scheduling from the analysis", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#workflow", "text": "1. Separate scheduling from the analysis \n The example uses two files: \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n File \n Where it runs \n What it does \n \n \n \n \n atlas-job.sbatch \n Ubuntu worker node \n Requests resources through  #SBATCH  directives, loads the container runtime, and starts the EL9 command. \n \n \n run-atlas.sh \n Inside the EL9 container \n Initializes ATLAS, selects the requested release, and runs a small PyROOT test. \n \n \n \n The key ATLAS option is  -r , which runs a command inside the container without opening an interactive prompt. The wrapper invokes the setup script directly: \n # Pattern used in the batch script; the variables are defined there.\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \\\n    -c el9 -r \"$unity_atlas_command\" \n This is the scripted equivalent of  setupATLAS -c el9 , followed by commands inside the container. Simply writing  setupATLAS -c el9  on one line and your analysis on the next does not put that next line inside the container. Keep the analysis in the command passed to  -r . \n The  US ATLAS benchmarking examples  use this non-interactive interface. Our wrapper adapts it to Unity's module and Slurm configuration. \n"}, {"title": "2. Prepare a shared job directory", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#prepare", "text": "2. Prepare a shared job directory \n Start from a  fresh Ubuntu login shell , outside an interactive ATLAS container. Replace  pi_YOUR_PI  with your actual Unity group directory, as in the first chapter: \n # Unity login node\nexport ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/pi_YOUR_PI/$USER/atlas\"\nmkdir -p \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/atlas-test\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/atlas-test\"\nmkdir -p logs\n\ncurl --fail --location --output atlas-job.sbatch \\\n    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/slurm/atlas-job.sbatch\ncurl --fail --location --output run-atlas.sh \\\n    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/slurm/run-atlas.sh \n You can also save the files directly:  atlas-job.sbatch  and  run-atlas.sh . Both belong in the directory from which you will submit. No executable permission is required here: Slurm reads the submission script, and the container runs the payload with  bash . \n Create  logs/  before submitting.  Slurm opens the output files before the script runs, so creating that directory inside the job would be too late. \n Slurm transfers the submission script, but it does  not  transfer your payload, analysis code, input files, or software build. They must remain accessible on shared storage. Avoid editing  run-atlas.sh  or its dependencies while jobs using them are queued or running; keep a separate copy for a new version. \n"}, {"title": "3. The worker-node script", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#batch-script", "text": "3. The worker-node script \n atlas-job.sbatch  requests one CPU, 4 GB of memory, and 15 minutes for the small test. Its login Bash shell initializes the host's module environment. It creates a separate  runs/JOB_ID/  directory for every submission. \n #!/bin/bash -l\n#SBATCH --job-name=atlas-test\n#SBATCH --partition=cpu\n#SBATCH --nodes=1\n#SBATCH --ntasks=1\n#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=1\n#SBATCH --mem=4G\n#SBATCH --time=00:15:00\n#SBATCH --output=logs/%x-%j.out\n#SBATCH --error=logs/%x-%j.err\n#SBATCH --export=NONE\n\n# This script runs on the allocated Ubuntu worker node.\nif [[ $# -ne 1 || $1 != *,* ]]; then\n    echo \"Usage: sbatch atlas-job.sbatch AnalysisBase,VERSION\" >&2\n    exit 2\nfi\nunity_atlas_release=$1\ncd \"${SLURM_SUBMIT_DIR:?Submit from your shared job directory}\" || exit 1\nunity_atlas_payload=\"$PWD/run-atlas.sh\"\nif [[ ! -r \"$unity_atlas_payload\" ]]; then\n    echo \"Cannot read payload: $unity_atlas_payload\" >&2\n    exit 1\nfi\nunity_atlas_run_dir=\"$PWD/runs/${SLURM_JOB_ID:?Missing Slurm job ID}\"\nmkdir -p \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" || exit 1\n\n# Load the container runtime on the host, before entering EL9.\nmodule load apptainer/latest || exit \"$?\"\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nif [[ ! -r \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" ]]; then\n    echo \"ATLASLocalRootBase is unavailable on $(hostname)\" >&2\n    exit 1\nfi\n\n# Enable NVIDIA passthrough only when this job has allocated GPUs.\nif [[ -n ${SLURM_JOB_GPUS:-} ]]; then\n    export APPTAINER_NV=true\n    if [[ -n ${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-} ]]; then\n        export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"$CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"\n    fi\nfi\n\n# Pass arguments explicitly; do not depend on host variables surviving\n# the container's environment filtering. %q quotes each Bash argument.\nprintf -v unity_atlas_command 'bash %q %q %q %q' \\\n    \"$unity_atlas_payload\" \"$unity_atlas_release\" \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" \\\n    \"${SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK:-1}\"\nprintf 'Job %s on %s; release %s; output %s\\n' \\\n    \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" \"$(hostname)\" \"$unity_atlas_release\" \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\"\ncd \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" || exit 1\n\n# -r runs the payload inside the container and then leaves the container.\n# /work is already visible on the tested Unity setup; no -m is needed.\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \\\n    -c el9 -r \"$unity_atlas_command\"\nunity_atlas_status=$?\nprintf 'Container command exited with status %s\\n' \"$unity_atlas_status\"\nexit \"$unity_atlas_status\" \n The wrapper sources ATLASLocalRootBase explicitly instead of depending on the interactive  setupATLAS  alias in  .bashrc . The  --export=NONE  directive avoids importing the submission shell's full environment; Slurm still supplies its job variables and may initialize the user's login environment. This is not a completely empty environment. Keep software and container startup out of your shell startup files, as described in the first chapter. \n /work  is already visible inside the tested Unity container, so the standard command needs no  -m /work . If you use another filesystem that is missing inside EL9, add the corresponding  -m /actual/path  to the container launch. \n This is a  single-node, single-process  job. Slurm already runs the batch script on the worker, so it does not need an additional  srun  for this example. MPI and multiple simultaneous tasks require a different launch pattern. If you later introduce  srun , note that this script uses  --export=NONE : use  srun --export=ALL ...  when the step needs the environment you have prepared. \n"}, {"title": "4. The script inside EL9", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#payload", "text": "4. The script inside EL9 \n run-atlas.sh  starts a fresh Bash process inside the container. It initializes the ATLAS command definitions, runs  asetup  for the release passed at submission, and creates  hist.root  without requiring any input data or credentials. \n #!/bin/bash\n# This script runs inside the EL9 container.\nunity_atlas_release=${1:?Missing ATLAS project and version}\nunity_atlas_run_dir=${2:?Missing output directory}\nunity_atlas_cpus=${3:-1}\ncd \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" || exit 1\n# Do not let a sourced setup script interpret the payload's arguments.\nset --\n\n# A new Bash process needs its own ATLAS command definitions.\n# ALRB defines aliases such as asetup; Bash scripts must enable them.\nshopt -s expand_aliases\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\"\nunity_atlas_status=$?\nif (( unity_atlas_status != 0 )); then\n    exit \"$unity_atlas_status\"\nfi\nasetup \"$unity_atlas_release\"\nunity_atlas_status=$?\nif (( unity_atlas_status != 0 )); then\n    exit \"$unity_atlas_status\"\nfi\n\n# Enable strict error handling after the external setup scripts finish.\nset -euo pipefail\nexport OMP_NUM_THREADS=\"$unity_atlas_cpus\"\nprintf 'Container OS:\\n'\ncat /etc/os-release\nprintf '\\nWorking directory: %s\\n' \"$PWD\"\n\n# Replace this smoke test with your analysis command.\npython -u - <<'PY'\nimport ROOT\n\nROOT.gROOT.SetBatch(True)\nROOT.gRandom.SetSeed(12345)\noutput = ROOT.TFile.Open(\"hist.root\", \"RECREATE\")\nif not output or output.IsZombie():\n    raise RuntimeError(\"Cannot create hist.root\")\nhist = ROOT.TH1D(\"example\", \"ATLAS on Unity; x; Entries\", 50, -5, 5)\nhist.FillRandom(\"gaus\", 10000)\nhist.Write()\noutput.Close()\nprint(\"ROOT version:\", ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())\nprint(\"Wrote hist.root with 10000 entries\")\nPY\ntest -s hist.root\nprintf 'Payload finished successfully.\\n' \n shopt -s expand_aliases  lets this non-interactive Bash script use the aliases defined by ATLASLocalRootBase, including  asetup . The setup and  asetup  commands are on separate lines so those definitions are available before the release command is parsed. \n External setup scripts run before strict Bash error handling is enabled; their return codes are checked explicitly. Once setup finishes,  set -euo pipefail  makes a failed analysis command stop the payload. The wrapper returns the container command's status to Slurm. When adapting the example, keep this error handling: a final successful  echo  must not hide a failed analysis. \n"}, {"title": "5. Submit and inspect the result", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#submit", "text": "5. Submit and inspect the result \n Use the  same project and exact release version  you successfully tested interactively, for example  AnalysisBase  followed by its version, separated by a comma. Do not enter a literal placeholder or a moving  latest  version. The prompt below runs on the login node; the batch job itself never prompts for input. \n # Still in the directory containing both scripts and logs/.\nread -r -p 'Tested ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" == *,* ]]; then\n    if JOB_ID=$(sbatch --parsable atlas-job.sbatch \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"); then\n        JOB_ID=${JOB_ID%%;*}\n        printf 'Submitted job: %s\\n' \"$JOB_ID\"\n    fi\nelse\n    echo 'Enter a project and exact version, separated by a comma.'\nfi \n Record the returned job ID. In a later login session, set  JOB_ID  to that number before using these commands: \n # Queued/running jobs; a completed job will disappear from squeue.\nsqueue --jobs=\"$JOB_ID\"\n\n# Accounting, including the batch step; may take a short time to update.\nsacct --jobs=\"$JOB_ID\" \\\n      --format=JobID,JobName,State,ExitCode,Elapsed,MaxRSS\n\n# These files appear once the job starts.\ntail -n 50 \"logs/atlas-test-${JOB_ID}.out\"\ntail -n 50 \"logs/atlas-test-${JOB_ID}.err\"\nls -lh \"runs/${JOB_ID}/hist.root\" \n For a successful smoke test, check  all three : \n \n Slurm reports  COMPLETED  with  ExitCode  equal to  0:0 . \n The output log identifies EL9, reports the ROOT version, and ends with the successful payload message and container status zero. \n runs/JOB_ID/hist.root  exists and is nonempty. In an interactive session with the same release, you can open it and inspect the histogram  example , which should have 10,000 entries. \n \n MaxRSS  is generally reported for a step such as  JOB_ID.batch ; keep those rows in the accounting output. Check both logs when a job fails. For Slurm, the first number in  ExitCode  is the return code and the second is the terminating signal. \n To cancel a queued or running job  when you want to stop it : \n scancel \"$JOB_ID\" \n"}, {"title": "6. Replace the test with your analysis", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#analysis", "text": "6. Replace the test with your analysis \n Replace the Python block and the  test -s hist.root  check in  run-atlas.sh  with your analysis command and an appropriate output check. Keep the release setup and run-directory change. Relative output paths then land in  runs/JOB_ID/ ; use absolute paths for shared inputs and code. \n For example, after changing these paths to your own: \n # Inside run-atlas.sh, after asetup and set -euo pipefail.\npython -u /work/pi_YOUR_PI/YOUR_USERNAME/atlas/analysis/run.py \\\n    --input /work/pi_YOUR_PI/shared/input.root \\\n    --output result.root\ntest -s result.root \n If your analysis uses a compiled ATLAS package, build it in an EL9 allocation with the same release and platform, then source that build's exact  setup.sh  after  asetup  in the payload. Keep the build accessible on shared storage. Do not build the same directory concurrently from multiple jobs. \n Remote data access and authentication need their own setup; having software on CVMFS does not grant access to protected data. The initial smoke test deliberately needs neither. \n Adjust CPU, memory, and time \n Command-line  sbatch  options override the corresponding  #SBATCH  directives. For example, once your analysis is configured to use four CPUs: \n sbatch --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \\\n    atlas-job.sbatch \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" \n Requesting four CPUs does not automatically parallelize Python or ROOT. The example passes the CPU allocation into the container and sets  OMP_NUM_THREADS ; configure your application's own thread or worker count as needed. Keep that count within the allocation.  --mem  here is memory per node, not per CPU. \n Keep directives at the top of the file, before shell commands. Slurm reads them before executing Bash, so  #SBATCH --output=$HOME/...  does not expand  $HOME . Use a literal path, a Slurm filename token such as  %j  (job ID), or an option on the  sbatch  command line. \n Request an NVIDIA GPU \n The same wrapper detects an allocated GPU through  SLURM_JOB_GPUS , enables Apptainer's NVIDIA support, and forwards Slurm's  CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES  selection when present. Request the GPU at submission: \n sbatch --partition=gpu --gpus=1 --cpus-per-task=4 \\\n    --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 atlas-job.sbatch \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" \n Add  nvidia-smi  after setup inside  run-atlas.sh  to inspect device visibility, then use a CUDA-capable application and compatible software environment. The provided histogram test runs on the CPU even when a GPU is allocated; it does not validate a GPU framework. Do not replace Slurm's device selection with a hard-coded GPU number. \n"}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#troubleshooting", "text": "Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n No log files yet \n A pending job has not started. Check  squeue ; also confirm  logs/  existed before submission and is writable. \n \n \n module: command not found \n Keep the login-shell shebang  #!/bin/bash -l ; check host shell initialization and Unity's module instructions. \n \n \n setupATLAS  or  asetup  is not found \n Use the supplied explicit setup and alias-expansion sequence; an interactive  .bashrc  alias is not enough for batch scripts. \n \n \n Script or input missing inside the container \n Submit from the shared job directory, keep the payload there, and verify its absolute path inside EL9. Add  -m  only for paths not already visible. \n \n \n Software setup fails \n Confirm the exact project, release, and platform worked in a fresh interactive EL9 session. Check compute-node CVMFS access. \n \n \n FAILED ,  OUT_OF_MEMORY , or  TIMEOUT \n Read both logs and the  sacct  state; fix the command or adjust the measured memory/time requirement. \n \n \n COMPLETED  but output is missing \n Check that the payload actually ran, did not hide a failed command, and wrote to the intended run directory. Keep explicit output checks. \n \n \n GPU unavailable inside the payload \n Check the GPU resource request, NVIDIA passthrough, Slurm's device selection, and the application's CUDA support. \n \n \n \n"}, {"title": "References", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#references", "text": "References \n \n Unity: batch jobs with sbatch  and  partitions . \n US ATLAS analysis-facility benchmarking examples , including ATLASLocalRootBase container commands and batch wrappers. \n ATLAS Software Documentation . \n Slurm sbatch ,  sacct , and  job exit codes . \n Apptainer NVIDIA GPU support . \n \n Documentation reviewed:  7 November 2026 . Interactive Unity setup:  tested by Rafael . This batch recipe:  local checks passed; end-to-end Unity test pending . \n"}];
